WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.430
When you think of cutting edge technology

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:09.193
at sea, you might be
thinking of stuff like this.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:13.900
But there's an incoming
revolution on the high seas

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.400
that isn't quite as sexy,

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.703
but could be significantly more impactful.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.390
Container shipping is the key
component of global trade.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:28.130
About 80 or 90% of all the
world's goods are transported

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.820
at sea at some point.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.830
And these ships carry trillions
of dollars of goods on them

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:36.367
and without them, there
would be no globalization.

00:00:36.367 --> 00:00:38.270
But there's a significant unseen cost

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.620
to the modern era of global commerce.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.570
About 3% of all the
world's CO2 emissions come

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:44.650
from shipping.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:46.820
3% may not sound like a lot,

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:50.140
but that's roughly comparable
to the entire CO2 output

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.760
of Germany, or in other
words around the same amount

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.690
of emissions from all
forms of aviation combined.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.290
Since reducing trade
isn't a likely option,

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:03.180
what about a technological
improvement to help reduce

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:06.033
the emissions from these
well-stocked maritime behemoths?

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.420
Transport by the sea
has always been the most

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.000
cost-efficient method of moving.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.190
It was like that in the Stone Age

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.273
and it's still like that today.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.183
In the more recent history
it was in the 1970s

00:01:33.183 --> 00:01:35.780
that the big breakthrough
came when the use

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.010
of the standard-sized
containers became the norm.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:40.990
That's really what revolutionized trade

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.633
and made globalization as
we know it today possible.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.370
Today, we are used to
buying things in Europe

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.073
and the U.S that's made in China.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.620
50 years ago, that was not the norm.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.853
That's the containers
that made that possible.

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:58.670
Only about six or 7,000
container ships handle

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:01.280
this big conveyor belt of goods.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.200
They have doubled in size many times over

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.750
during the last couple of decades

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.890
and now the biggest container
ships there are can carry

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:10.730
more than 20,000 containers

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.083
and they're longer than 400 meters.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.860
So why not electrify
a fleet of cargo ships

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.810
and take advantage of the
emissions cuts that come with it?

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.730
Well, some are trying.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.690
Take Yara, a fertilizer company in Norway,

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.190
and their electric autonomous
ship, The Birkeland.

00:02:32.646 --> 00:02:33.950
And we can start by
addressing the question,

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:37.290
why is a fertilizer company
building ships at all?

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.790
We have a lot of focus of
making sustainable products,

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.810
producing food for the whole population

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:47.840
in the sustainable way
is one of important tasks

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:49.730
that we as a company see.

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:53.680
And transportation and the
supply chain is also part

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.500
of the environmental load
that we put on the society.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.320
The Birkeland, it's unique
because it is electric

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.480
and then also emission-free and
it's going to be autonomous,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.322
without any ship crew on board.

00:03:09.322 --> 00:03:14.280
We are exporting about
20,000 containers a year,

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.880
so that will then eliminate
40,000 truck drives

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:20.380
through the town of Porsgrunn.

00:03:21.624 --> 00:03:23.310
The Birkeland isn't
going to be any cheaper

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.570
to operate than a traditional
vessel, at least for now.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.070
But according to Yara,
that's not its purpose.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.120
It is more the effect on the environment,

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.040
reducing CO2 emission and
also an important factor

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.590
that this ship has got a lot of attention

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:42.100
and it's been the kind of a
catalyst for the others also

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.243
to develop green maritime transport.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.460
But the Yara Birkeland has

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.590
a significant limitation, range.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.850
A similarly-sized vessel with
a diesel engine can travel

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:02.763
up to 5,000 nautical miles,
the Yara Birkeland, around 80.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.940
They will use it for a very
specific route in Norway

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.860
between two sites fairly
close to each other.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:14.280
The ship can carry about 120 containers

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.900
and you would need about 200
of these small ships to match

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:19.770
one of the very large container vessels

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:21.350
that's out out there.

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.360
So the Yara Birkeland has a solid,

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.373
but niche potential application.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.360
For longer range ships, the
technology faces the same issues

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:32.320
that were common to early road-going EVs.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.130
If you look at a Tesla,

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:37.720
Tesla has a battery that
weighs 1,200 pounds.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.460
If you should fit a battery
into a container ship

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:44.503
that's 400 meters long,
it would just be massive.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.440
Because of these limitations,

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.480
the industry's heavy hitters
are turning elsewhere

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:53.820
for carbon cutting.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.930
Maersk, the world's
largest shipping company

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.380
by vessels owned, currently
consumes around 12 million tons

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.200
of oil per year, which is

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.460
about what the entire
world produces every day.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:08.030
In order to reach its goal of
being carbon neutral by 2040,

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:10.780
the company is looking to
change up what's in the tank.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.390
The power requirements are just too high

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.380
for battery technology at the moment.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.950
You're talking about
ships with an engine on

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:20.110
with more than a hundred
thousand horse power.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.720
In the marine industry,
the only real way to reduce

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.183
emissions is to actually change the fuel.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.202
To begin that change, Maersk turned to

00:05:32.202 --> 00:05:33.680
MAN Solutions.

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:36.210
MAN Energy Solutions is
part of the Volkswagen group

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.460
and the oldest industrial
company in Germany founded

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.260
in 1758.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.820
We're a little bit different
to the car industry

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.830
because we operate in the marine industry.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:46.980
With A.P. Moller-Maersk,
we'll receive an order

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.920
for 12 methanol fuel ships.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:52.020
So the idea is to have
a carbon neutral ship,

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.070
completely carbon neutral.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.770
Methanol's quite an interesting fuel

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:58.783
because, of course, it's very CO2 neutral.

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:03.660
The second thing is it's
liquified at ambient temperature,

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:07.080
so actually it's relatively
simple to store and use.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.720
the genius thing about the
engines that we produce is

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:12.440
because of our modular
design, we can actually modify

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.540
the engines fairly simply
to run on different fuels.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.520
And actually, a lot of
our engines are supplied

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:18.960
in a dual fuel mode,

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.307
so they can run on one fuel today

00:06:20.307 --> 00:06:22.230
and a different fuel tomorrow.

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.680
There's many types of new
fuels that will allow us

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:26.400
to decarbonize the industry.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.000
But of course, what we need
is a kind of transition

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.420
and you can go to liquid natural gas

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.630
or even synthetic natural
gas, which give us

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.950
good reductions in CO2.

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:41.130
We intend to have a vessel
operating in 2024 on ammonia,

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.820
which, of course, is the
absolute game changer

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.193
in terms of emissions.

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:51.500
Some of these alternative fuels,

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:53.660
however, have drawbacks.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:54.900
We're looking at ammonia,

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:57.380
which is a highly toxic chemical,

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:59.263
but a it's also CO2 neutral.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:02.330
Obviously the disadvantage
is that it's very dangerous

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.123
both for humans and marine life.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.880
Liquified natural gas, some
shipping lines are looking

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:08.713
at that.

00:07:08.713 --> 00:07:09.700
The problem with that
is obviously that it's

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:13.790
not CO2 neutral, it just
reduces the CO2 emissions

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:14.653
from the ships.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.440
But another solution is methanol,

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.410
which is sourced from green electricity.

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:24.910
Methanol is readily available today

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.010
and really is carbon neutral.

00:07:27.010 --> 00:07:30.260
Of all the technologies, I
think methanol is for sure

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:33.410
the short or medium-term future.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:35.100
There's an infrastructure around methanol.

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.670
I think methanol, at the moment,
seems to be more available

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:40.670
and, of course, the technology's proven.

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:42.343
Although switching

00:07:42.343 --> 00:07:45.380
to alternative fuels is
expected to add 10 to 12%

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.910
to shipping costs, this
could be the perfect time

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:50.910
for shipping companies to add
in a buffer for that premium.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.300
In the last two years,
the industry has been

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:54.133
in great shape.

00:07:54.133 --> 00:07:56.310
And the reason why
they're in great shape is

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:57.890
while many are aware of
that the world suffers

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:01.080
from supply chain issues, but
it's actually an advantage

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.770
for the companies that own the ships

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.660
because they can charge
higher prices at the moment.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:07.840
So there's a lot of cash flow

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.470
in the container shipping
industry right now

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:11.753
and that should enable the companies

00:08:11.753 --> 00:08:14.690
and the ship owners to
invest in new technologies

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:16.473
that reduce global emissions.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:18.680
While these mammoth vessels make

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:21.180
incremental progress in decarbonizing,

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.283
smaller ships are more readily
adopting new technologies.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:33.903
Take Promare's Mayflower vessel.

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:38.070
Using hybrid solar, wind,
and diesel propulsion,

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:41.490
it's equipped to travel
autonomously with no crew to conduct

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.083
research on marine health.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.610
It's designed to cruise around the oceans

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:48.200
and collect scientific data.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:50.940
So climate data, water quality data,

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.990
data about marine mammals,
and bring back samples

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:56.200
and also transmit samples off the boat

00:08:57.432 --> 00:09:00.382
into the ether for consumption
by the scientific community.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.410
According to Promare, the advantages

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:06.770
of the Mayflower being
unmanned and AI driven will be

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:08.900
both in its increased efficiency

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:11.770
and its ability to go
where most ships never do.

00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:12.920
One of the things to bear in mind

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:16.130
with these vessels is once you
remove all the people stuff,

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:18.120
they become extremely efficient

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.770
in the sense that the power
it takes to move this vessel

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:23.500
over long distances is
absurdly low by comparison

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:26.063
to a vessel the same size
carrying people on it.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:30.430
It's very, very green because
it just sips on the fuel,

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:33.270
but it's a dual electric
motors that are driven

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:35.440
by lithium iron phosphate battery systems,

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.280
similar to what you'd find in the Tesla.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.430
And then there are
solar panels that charge

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:42.160
emergency batteries and then
also charge the main batteries

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.960
so that if we lose main
propulsion for some reason,

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:47.350
the solar power and battery
systems that are adjacent

00:09:47.350 --> 00:09:49.900
to the primary batteries will
keep the navigation, lights,

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:53.090
and communications and position data live.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:57.050
Then the main batteries that
drive the engines and run

00:09:57.050 --> 00:10:00.360
all the sophisticated systems
are periodically charged

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.230
by a biodiesel generator.

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:04.410
So we can use biofuel or
we can use straight diesel,

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:05.523
whichever we prefer.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:12.410
It doesn't replace
ships with people on it,

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.920
but it sort of democratized
the ability to collect data

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:18.560
and produce information
that we can all share

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:20.880
so that when we do send
a very expensive ship

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.200
with people on it out
for very long voyages

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.380
into the middle of nowhere,

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.190
that those people are
best spending their time

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:30.050
on the areas that will
produce the most results

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:31.780
in terms of answering the big questions

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:34.260
that boats like this help to ask.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.650
The dream would be to have many,
many, many ships like this.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:40.010
So if you look at where we
get our data about the ocean,

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:41.600
it's almost completely biased

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:42.840
to the commercial shipping channels,

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.320
ships going back and forth
on major trading routes.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.500
Ships like Mayflower
and many others like it,

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:52.280
both very small ones, even
larger ones are what we need

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:54.560
to kind of reduce the cost of collecting

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.180
that fundamental data and producing

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:58.233
that sort of first tier of information.

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:01.320
Promare plans to send the Mayflower

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:03.903
on its first trans-Atlantic
voyage in April.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.450
Across the pond in Alameda, California,

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:20.480
another company is
looking to commit swarms

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:23.350
of electric autonomous drones to the seas.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.760
That company is Saildrone.

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:26.900
The first vehicles were designed to be

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:29.720
data-gathering robots that
really gather atmospheric

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.880
and oceanographic data, which
is kinda the critical piece

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:34.010
of the puzzle we need to
understand climate change

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:35.660
and the rate of change.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:38.010
So we started gathering
data on fish stocks,

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:40.270
on water column itself.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:43.300
That led us to ocean mapping,
so mapping the sea bed.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.920
What we excel at with the
robot is kind of the long,

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:47.451
monotonous, or dangerous things

00:11:47.451 --> 00:11:48.860
that people shouldn't be doing.

00:11:48.860 --> 00:11:52.040
So persistent present
operations in the high Arctic,

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:53.660
Southern Ocean, where
it's just too rough to go

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:54.910
with a human.

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:56.960
Hurricane exploration is a great example.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:11:59.850
The boats are electric
vehicles so they run

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:01.450
on battery power.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:04.070
It is wind-powered
propulsion and solar-powered

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:07.020
for the electricity for
the computers and so on.

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:10.040
Electrification of marine systems is key,

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:11.750
but I'll go further than that saying

00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:12.710
it's not just electrification,

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:16.430
it's harnessing renewable sources
of wind and solar to power

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:17.263
those vehicles along.

00:12:17.263 --> 00:12:19.990
The real challenge for any
maritime vessel is having

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:21.597
enough power on board to propel you.

00:12:21.597 --> 00:12:24.010
So if you don't harvest
power along the way,

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:25.810
you have to carry that as stored power,

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:27.520
either a huge amount of batteries

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:30.960
or an alternative fuel source,
diesel, hydrogen, or other.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.290
So for us, we try and maximize each fuel

00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:35.750
to renewable sources and
obviously that's wind,

00:12:35.750 --> 00:12:37.410
sailing ships, kind of one
of the oldest technologies

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.350
on the planet and a true, reliable,

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:40.530
and sustainable form of power.

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.520
So we utilize that a lot

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:44.600
and that reduces the amount
of fuel you have to take

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:46.280
and it reduces the
amount of mechanical load

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:47.300
on the vehicles.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:48.890
Less time you can run an engine for,

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:50.340
the longer that's gonna last.

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:52.910
But there are areas of the
world where you don't have

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:53.810
a lot of sunlight.

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:59.123
Northern hemisphere in the
winter and up in the Arctic.

00:12:59.123 --> 00:12:59.956
Obviously, a little bit's frozen,

00:12:59.956 --> 00:13:01.830
but in the unfrozen area
where where we operate,

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:04.070
you have very very low light levels.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:06.100
Very short days, very short summers.

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:09.280
And that means we struggle for
power in the winter months.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.180
Right now we're using
high-efficiency small diesel engines

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:15.120
that run at kind of a very
efficient RPM to charge batteries

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:16.120
in a very short period of time.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:18.210
So like a diesel-electric system on a ship

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:20.240
where you use engines to charge batteries

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:22.240
and then you run the ship off batteries.

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:25.660
Having backup combustion engines proves

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:27.830
just how hard it will be
for ocean-going vessels

00:13:27.830 --> 00:13:30.163
to completely ditch carbon-emitting fuels.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:34.810
Regardless, stakeholders in
the industry seem to agree

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:38.080
that progress must continue and fast.

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:40.670
If we can decarbonize this industry

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:42.470
and we need to act very, very quickly

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:44.940
because it's not just about the new ships,

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:46.350
it's about the existing ships,

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:48.730
then we can have a dramatic
impact to make sure

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:51.330
we hit the targets that been set out.

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:53.790
We needed to act yesterday, not tomorrow,

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:54.920
we needed to act yesterday.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:57.460
So the quicker we can get this moving,

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:58.960
then the better for everybody.

